From c1e9e45cb03c615ead8644e39506e376cb55af9d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 08:40:40 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 41/88] sniff: a panicking sniffer fails its connection, a UDP
 sniff reads 16 packets

Sniffers parse whatever a client in the LAN sends, and nothing recovered a
panic on that path: one bug on unexpected input (the ja3 bounds fixed in the
previous backport were one) took the whole process down, and with it the DNS
of the LAN. PeekStream and PeekPacket now recover a sniffer's panic: the sniff
of that connection fails with ErrSnifferPanicked, the metadata the sniffer had
set is restored, and the router logs the panic with its stack as an error.

A UDP sniff kept reading while the sniffer asked for more data, each packet
held in a buffer of its own (16 KiB), as long as every packet came within the
timeout. QUIC Initials whose CRYPTO frames never complete a ClientHello ran it
to the 64 KiB bound of the reassembly: 66 packets held per session in the
test. The sniff now ends after 16 packets with an error later sniffs do not
resume from; a ClientHello takes one or two Initials.
